or a show called black sails  full app so we couldn't use the  beautiful studio so we built in  warehouses and I'm thrilled that  Michaels show is out there employing  lots of people but we built in this  really not very attractive warehouse and  then we built this bunker in there and I  remember Helen coming off after about  day three and going Gavin I can't  imagine what it's like to do this every  single day to come to work as the person  we based her character on does and do  this job every single day she was a  little shaken after 12 hours of shooting  inside this bunker you shot in South  Africa before  which won an academy award for best  foreign language film thank you and you  shot this film there but you also had a  Somali cast don't tell us about how that  came to pass well logistically it proved  to be too tricky to try and do this in  Kenya for many reasons Kenya's a great  country in security foremost among  curity exactly and so we went to Cape  Town where there is a tremendous filmic  infrastructure great cruise many of whom  I've worked with before and there  happens to be a very very large Somali  refugee community in cape town that is  whole neighborhoods of their own people  who have fled al-shabaab and so every  actor and indeed every extra you see in  the film is a Somali refugee every  Somali actor so admin hajiya who plays  the young girl's father fled because his  brother was shot by al-shabaab frankly  and and when you talk to these folks we  went into I have an amazing South  African casting director moon in li and  when we went into these Somali  communities and had open castings some  people came in and heard what the gist  of the story was and heard that  al-shabaab was in some way involved in  the story turn in you know what I mean  as characters in the film and walked  right out just out of fear so the folks  who stay have amazing stories that  little girl is a refugee her mother and  her little bro little real-life little  brothers the little boy who runs to her  at the end and for the bread that's her  a real brother and her mum and her other  sister are just two of the characters  that come to buy bread from her at one  point and they have escaped across five  countries to get to Cape Town I'm so  amazing amazing people it makes you very  humble when you're working with the very  often at the end of films that deal with  military subjects you say we want to  thank the United States Navy for its  cooperation I didn't see anything like  that here so has the NSA come knocking  on your door yet probably I will say  this they'd never let you know no that's  you great question um we did not  approach the  ntaganda film office or the Air Force  film officers and they exist and and not  because we don't want to we just felt  that this film needed to be made without  the endorsement of anyone you also don't  see the endorsement of Amnesty  International or reprieve all of whom we  consulted we consulted with different  organizations we consulted with people  in the military and one and I did  extensive research of people in military  many of the lines that you see in the  film come directly from the mouths of  people in military i'll give you one  great example when aaron paul says i am  the pilot-in-command responsible for  releasing the weapon I i need the  collateral i will not i need you to run  the collateral damage estimate again i  will not release my weapon until that  happens that is a direct line from the  colonel who trained the girl islets who  by the way emailed me and congratulating  me on the film the other day i think he  was very nervous and didn't want to be  named some of their names are in the  special thanks without rank but others  are not and and the reason for that is  that there is a kind of last line of  defense that a drone pilot has and we  did have military advisors on the set  the pilot that that was on the set  advise erin was a f-16 pilot for 10  years he flew in the iraq war and he has  been flying drones for six years and now  is in the reserve so he still flies but  he consults on movies and he flies um  drones civilian drones for movies with  cameras so he was a great guy and that  was useful because for the actors you  want them to feel that they know that  when they push this button or move the  sleeve or touch that autopilot or read  the dash 34 checklist that they are  absolutely doing the right thing today  as the ghost of Nuremberg haunts this  film yes but you want them to know just  as actors that they're doing the right  thing so that they can get the technical  stuff out of the way and get back to  acting but to your point of that goes to  Nuremberg yes thank you for raising that  the question for an aaron paul and it  happened to our advisor which is where  we got the line  is as a young officer in the military  you do not promise to obey all orders  you swear to obey all legal orders and  this is fundamental because if you obey  an illegal order as you point out you  can be charged with a warm crime if that  were not the case they could be no  Nuremberg so the pressure on a 24 25  year old pilot if he thinks that this is  an illegal order for any reason he is  actually obliged to say so now many of  us will say I bet they hardly ever do  and I bet we'd be right but they can and  part of what I got from the colonel who  trained the pilots was an immense  frustration at the point that he was  working bear in mind this was already  years ago when we were doing the  research because the CIA was using air  force pilots in some of their operations  and those Air Force pilots their names  are known their ranks are known they  can't hide behind this anonymous they're  not anonymity so if a drone strike turns  out to have been illegally in any way  who's on the hook so it was amazing to  me to find that within the military  itself there was a great deal of dispute  some people are incredibly Pro these  drones some people in the military  described them as humana humane as  weapons and we can talk about that if  you like but and others are this is  really a tactical weapon and we should  not confuse it with a strategy and  whether we're using them strategically  correctly or not is a huge debate in  terms of the blowback question won't you  please thank Gavin hood thank you so  much for coming folks really appreciate  it thank you
 

